1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,300
[On screen] Hi, I'm Param Madan. I built DEFX Bridge, an independent R-based prototype inspired by the DEFX work presented at PharmaSUG 2026.

2
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:14,032
In clinical programming, every derivation is explained twice: once in the code, and again, by hand, in the define.xml metadata.

3
00:00:14,082 --> 00:00:17,677
What if the comment beside the code could be the metadata?

4
00:00:19,127 --> 00:00:24,080
That's the DEFX approach, published by Brendan Bartley and Megan Hinger.

5
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:26,999
DEFX Bridge is my independent R prototype.

6
00:00:27,049 --> 00:00:33,114
It reads the same tags from SAS and from R, and normalizes both into one metadata table.

7
00:00:34,564 --> 00:00:37,369
Here is a real scan of three synthetic programs.

8
00:00:37,419 --> 00:00:39,926
Forty-eight tag lines become forty records.

9
00:00:39,976 --> 00:00:43,911
Nothing is executed; the R language's own parser reads the R files.

10
00:00:43,961 --> 00:00:49,383
Each record keeps its dataset, variable and type, and the exact file and lines it came from.

11
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:51,941
Now a real mistake.

12
00:00:51,991 --> 00:00:55,718
This tag points at a variable that isn't in the specification.

13
00:00:55,768 --> 00:01:03,029
Validation catches it, names the file and the line, and exits with code two, so nothing downstream can quietly continue.

14
00:01:04,479 --> 00:01:07,202
I correct the reference in a copy of the file.

15
00:01:07,252 --> 00:01:08,830
The difference is one line.

16
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:12,749
The same command now passes, with zero errors and exit code zero.

17
00:01:14,199 --> 00:01:16,812
Export fills a copy of a define-like workbook.

18
00:01:16,862 --> 00:01:19,706
The three tag lines become one method description.

19
00:01:19,756 --> 00:01:24,859
The stale origin on that variable becomes Derived, with a method reference that resolves.

20
00:01:24,909 --> 00:01:29,317
Hand-entered cells, including the expression code, stay exactly as they were.

21
00:01:30,767 --> 00:01:38,682
Before the file is kept, it is read back with a different library and compared with the template, cell by cell: eighteen checks, all passing.

22
00:01:38,732 --> 00:01:43,879
Sixty-six automated tests pass locally and on GitHub's CI, where nothing is allowed to skip.

23
00:01:45,329 --> 00:01:46,141
DEFX Bridge.

24
00:01:46,191 --> 00:01:53,536
One documentation convention across SAS and R, so a study can adopt R gradually, one program at a time.

25
00:01:53,586 --> 00:01:59,423
An independent research prototype, built with credit to the original DEFX authors.
